From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: HourlySword <hourlysword.dev@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 17:41:40 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] FunKey S build option, and headless measurements of the logic

ISAAC_FUNKEY (CMake) builds upstream's Linux program for the FunKey S (one Cortex-A7 core, 64 MB of RAM, no GPU):
the PSP's lean SWF loading (FL_LEAN) and 363x272 stage (genpsp/), the PSP's memory limits and budgets (parsed shapes
5 MB with eviction, short sounds 2 MB, decoded SWF bitmaps trimmed to 1.5 MB after every tick), no helper threads
(platformPoolWorkers / platformRasterServiceWorkers 0, as on the PSP), 24 sound channels, the PSP's leaf-cache rules,
no OpenGL, SDL2 and zlib linked statically (the FunKey's sysroot has SDL2 as a static library only; firmwares differ
in the libraries they ship).

musl has no backtrace(): the stack sampling (ISAAC_SAMPLE_TICK) and ISAAC_RNGTRACE's call sites are glibc only.

For measuring the logic on a one-core device, headless mode gains:
- ISAAC_SNAPSHOT=1 (with --norender): the snapshot a renderer would get is built every tick and dropped, since on
  one core it is made on the logic's time;
- ISAAC_TICKMARKS=1: "OFKMEM begin N" before and "OFKMEM tick N ents E ms T cpu C objs O" after the timed part of
  every tick (wall-clock and thread CPU time, enemies, GC objects); qemu's armprof plugin records the instruction
  count at each such line, so the instructions between the two are exactly what the timing covers;
- g_shapeTick follows the logic tick (shape eviction keeps what was used in the last 2 s);
- the run's peak and final memory (VmHWM, VmRSS, RssAnon, RssFile) at the end, on Linux;
- ISAAC_GCMINLIVE / ISAAC_GCGROWTH override the collector's thresholds.

Checked: the FunKey build plays the same as the lean PC build built with the same maths functions (musl 1.2.1's),
every enemy's state at every tick of 12,580 (tboi-funkeys scripts/measure/same-game.sh). On the console at 1296 MHz
the logic takes 9.9 ms per tick on average, 12.8 ms at the 90th percentile; peak memory 37.8 MB.
